From 1035507859e6666d6ff61645a125fbb553dcdc2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-perf <perf@localhost>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 22:23:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] x86: harden flags_written_mask against untrimmed input;
 pin the SDM masks

Red-team of my own flag-walk work found two gaps.

1. SILENT DEGRADATION ON UNTRIMMED INPUT.  flags_written_mask splits the
   line on spaces and matches the first token, so "    stc" yielded "",
   missed every arm, and returned the fail-closed F_ALL.  Every current
   caller passes an already-trimmed line, so this was harmless today -- but
   the failure is SILENT: a caller passing a raw line would simply see its
   folds stop happening with no diagnostic anywhere.  One trim at the top
   removes the class.  Found by a probe test printing the mask for nine
   input spellings, not by reading the code.

2. THE MASKS WERE UNDOCUMENTED AND UNTESTED.  The SDM claims behind them
   (stc/clc/cmc define CF alone; sahf loads SF/ZF/AF/PF/CF and does NOT
   affect OF; cld/std touch only DF) were prose in a comment, not tests.  A
   later edit could narrow a mask -- say, giving shld a small mask -- and
   the result would be an unsoundness, not a missed fold.  The new test
   asserts the SDM, not the implementation: shld/shrd/bt*/popfq/popf and
   every unrecognised mnemonic stay F_ALL, and the three narrow families
   get exactly their documented bits.  Operand and indentation forms are
   covered too, which is how gap 1 was caught.

Also documented the scope limit the narrow masks depend on: DF is NOT
modelled.  cld/std are masked 0, and a pushfq after a cld and after a std
really do differ in bit 10, so this is sound only because every rewrite
guarded by this module changes arithmetic flags alone.  A rewrite that
changed DF would need a seventh bit, and stating that beats leaving it to
be inferred.

NON-VACUITY: reverting the precise walk makes
a_cf_only_setter_does_not_hide_a_lahf_from_the_zero_compare_fold FAIL, which
is the test that demonstrates the shipped dead_writes.rs defect.  My first
attempt at that revert silently matched nothing -- cargo fmt had collapsed
the multi-line call -- and the suite stayed green, which is exactly the
false-negative this project's gates keep producing.  Checking the match
count instead of trusting the edit is what caught it.

cargo test --lib: 3911 passed, 0 failed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
